For her first month as the council's noise officer, Leila Haddad did exactly what the job description said: she stood beside busy roads with a decibel meter and wrote down the numbers. The numbers were supposed to show her where residents suffered most. Instead they produced a puzzle. The roaring junction outside the railway station attracted almost no complaints, whereas a quiet side street in the old quarter, measured at barely half the volume, generated dozens.

{{41}}

Daily averages, of course, hide this kind of thing completely. A shudder lasting ten seconds vanishes into twenty-four hours of otherwise calm readings. So Haddad put the meter away and began walking the old quarter at dawn and at midnight with a pair of microphones, noting not how loud things were but how often they broke the quiet.

{{42}}

What she needed was a way of making the pattern visible at a glance. Her answer, which she calls the sound calendar, was a simple chart: time ran along the bottom, and every interruption appeared as a small coloured square, with a different colour for machinery, traffic and voices.

{{43}}

Nobody on the committee needed it explained. Within a month, deliveries to the area were restricted to the hours after seven, and complaints fell by more than half.

{{44}}

Haddad's response was characteristically practical. Rather than defend the rule, she went back to her microphones and recorded the lorries themselves. It turned out that the real offenders were the engines left running and the reversing alarms, not the deliveries as such.

{{45}}

The method has since spread. Haddad's calendar is now used in a dozen towns, and a university has asked her to turn it into a free app that residents can use to log disturbances themselves.

{{46}}
